         0.2     11        0.3225      2.1904e-07        [alt]
         0.5     11          10.0      9.7694e-08        [canonical]
         0.5     11          10.0      1.0740e-07        [alt]
      -> the point-mass 1/x artefact is gone: R -> 0 as r -> 0, the maximum sits at
         x = 0.03101 (canonical) / 0.03101 (alt), and it is five to six orders under the 0.10 line.
      The scan is monotone in a_h: the more concentrated the baryons, the larger the inner
      peak, and the point mass is the (unphysical, divergent) limit of that sequence.
      a_h/r_M = 0.02 is a 0.24 kpc Hernquist scale at log M_b 11 -- far more concentrated
      than any real galaxy (a_h/r_M ~ 0.1 to 0.3); it is reported as the upper bound.

======================================================================================
E.  What sigma_t^2/c^2 would be needed to carry the amount (dominant energy condition)
======================================================================================
  A medium of mass M_med with p_t = rho sigma_t^2 carries, in the convergence,
  (sigma_t^2/2c^2) M_med.  Carrying F x M_b therefore needs sigma_t^2/c^2 = 2 F M_b/M_med.
  (The Tolman/active channel, factor 1 not 1/2, halves this; it is still >> 1.)

    what must be carried          M_med = 5.41 M_b      M_med = 1.4 M_b
    M_c at 10 r_M  =  9.51 M_b             3.52 c^2             13.59 c^2
    M_c at 30 r_M  = 29.50 M_b            10.91 c^2             42.14 c^2
    M_c at 100 r_M = 99.50 M_b            36.78 c^2            142.14 c^2
    KiDS share X_s = 59.50 M_b            22.00 c^2             85.00 c^2
  A medium with p = rho sigma^2 obeys the dominant energy condition |p| <= rho c^2 only for
  sigma^2 <= c^2.  The numbers above are all far above 1: carrying 9.51 M_b needs 3.5 c^2,
  the KiDS share needs 22 c^2 with the whole cosmic budget as the medium and 85 c^2 with
  the charge that actually falls in.  Those are factors 3.5 to 85 of |p| > rho c^2 -- a
  superluminal, dominant-energy-condition-violating medium -- not a correction to a galaxy.
  (Read the other way: with |p| <= rho c^2 the stress can carry AT MOST the mass it belongs
  to, i.e. at most 1x.  The 5x to 20x is outside the theory, not outside this lane.)

  E1  the actual value, for comparison (log M_b, both footings):
      log M_b    v_inf [km/s]      sigma_t^2/c^2        lensing correction
                 can      alt      can        alt       can        alt
       9            59.4       62.3    1.961e-08  2.156e-08    9.805e-09  1.078e-08
      10           105.6      110.7    6.201e-08  6.817e-08    3.101e-08  3.409e-08
      11           187.8      196.9    1.961e-07  2.156e-07    9.805e-08  1.078e-07
      12           333.9      350.1    6.201e-07  6.817e-07    3.101e-07  3.409e-07
      13           593.7      622.5    1.961e-06  2.156e-06    9.805e-07  1.078e-06
      14          1055.8     1107.0    6.201e-06  6.817e-06    3.101e-06  3.409e-06
      15          1877.5     1968.5    1.961e-05  2.156e-05    9.805e-06  1.078e-05
      (the lensing column is sigma_t^2/(2c^2) = v_inf^2/(4c^2), the deep-field value;
       the Tolman/active column is twice it.  At 200 km/s: 2.23e-07 and 1.11e-07.)
      Task statement "about 1e-6 for a 200 km/s galaxy": the asymptotic value is 2.2e-7;
       1e-6 is reached in the inner halo (x ~< 0.3, see the profile table) and at
       log M_b ~ 13. Both are reported; neither is within 5 orders of 10 per cent.

======================================================================================
F.  What sigma_r^2 < 0 requires of the medium, and the check against M5's three hypotheses
======================================================================================
  F1  WHAT IT REQUIRES.  Static, spherically symmetric, T^i_j = diag(p_r, p_t, p_t):

    (a) A KINETIC GAS IS EXCLUDED, EXACTLY.  For any one-particle distribution f >= 0,
        p_r = INT f v_r^2 d^3v >= 0.  The computed sigma_r^2 = -v_inf^2/2 < 0 is unreachable.
    (b) A FLUID AT REST IS EXCLUDED, EXACTLY.  A perfect fluid at rest has p_r = p_t.  A viscous
        fluid's shear stress is proportional to the rate of strain, which vanishes in a static
        configuration; holding p_r - p_t = -2 rho sigma_t^2 at rest and for ever therefore
        requires a STATIC SHEAR STRESS: a non-zero shear modulus mu != 0, i.e. AN ELASTIC SOLID.
    (c) THE STRESS STATE.  p_r = -p_t has mean p_bar = (p_r+2p_t)/3 = +p_t/3 (net pressure) and
        deviatoric part (2 p_t/3) diag(-2, 1, 1): a uniaxial TENSION along r held against
        tangential COMPRESSION.  That is a pressure-vessel / tensegrity state: radial cables in
        tension, tangential hoops in compression -- a NETWORK OF RADIAL FILAMENTS (or a solid
        with the equivalent anisotropic moduli).  A gas, a liquid and dust are all excluded.
    (d) HOW SOFT THE SOLID MUST BE (computed below): mu ~ p_t/3 ~ rho_h v_inf^2/6, so the
        transverse sound speed c_T = sqrt(mu/rho_h) ~ 0.41 v_inf.
    (e) THE TENSION ITSELF (computed below): the inward force transmitted across a sphere is
        4 pi r^2 |Pi_rr| -> 0.5000 x M_b a0 in the deep field, a CONSTANT (independent of r),
        equal to the baryons' own weight M_b g(r) at x = 2.7297 r_M (computed) -- i.e. at
        r ~ 33 kpc for log M_b = 11.  A cable can only pull: the network therefore has to be
        anchored at its inner end, and whatever it is anchored to takes a constant inward pull of
        half a M_b a0.  Anchored on the baryons, that is a new, non-gravitational force on
        baryons, which the law does not have and which the record's media do not couple
        through (M5's scope is "media coupling to baryons only through gravity").
      computed: 4 pi r^2 |Pi_rr| -> 0.5000 M_b a0 in the deep field (a constant), and it
      equals the baryons' own weight M_b g(r) at x = 2.7297 r_M  (r ~ 33 kpc at log M_b = 11)
  F2  numbers, canonical footing, log M_b = 11, at x = 10:
      rho_h = 2.960e-24 kg/m^3   Pi_tt = 5.397e-14 Pa   Pi_rr = -5.049e-14 Pa
      shear modulus mu ~ Pi_tt/3 = 1.799e-14 Pa   ->  c_T = sqrt(mu/rho_h) = 78.0 km/s = 0.415 v_inf
      tension force 4 pi r^2 |Pi_rr| = 8.9988e+30 N = 0.4833 x M_b a0   (deep field: M_b a0/2)
      Jeans: 4 pi G rho_h r^2 = 3.5207e+10 (m/s)^2  vs  c_T^2 = 6.0784e+09  ->  ratio 5.79  (>1: unstable)
